            <div xml:id="d680">

                <pb facs="0671" n="659" sameAs="frus:frus1937v01/pg659"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A19/103: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Italy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Phillips</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Rome</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-05-29T19:00:00+01:00">May 29, 1937—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received May 29—3:49 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>252. I read to Count Ciano this afternoon the substance of your telegram No. 92,
                    May 27, 7 p.m. pointing out that the Department considered it unfortunate that
                    some papers had given the impression that the American Government was not
                    receptive to the Duce’s suggestions. <pb facs="0672" n="660" xml:id="pg660"/>
                    Ciano was very gratified by this information. It is evident that Ciano himself
                    was not fully advised by the Duce of the step contemplated in the Simms
                    interview. He admitted frankly that he had only been aware of the proposal in
                    its most general terms and he had had no opportunity to discuss it fully with
                    the Duce which he would do as soon as the latter returned to Rome next week.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Phillips</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
